105
00:05:54.884 --> 00:06:02.329
5M per hour at peak (~1,400 per second), each with 1,000s of candidate properties.

106
00:06:02.609 --> 00:06:05.895
Look-to-book ratio: ~1,000:1 or higher.

107
00:06:06.175 --> 00:06:09.140
Bookings: ~1.5M room nights a day.

108
00:06:10.490 --> 00:06:11.877
Estimation highlights:

109
00:06:12.157 --> 00:06:13.181
Search cost.

110
00:06:13.461 --> 00:06:30.314
One search in a big city (for example, Goa for 3 nights, 2 adults) must: filter thousands of properties by availability on every night;. price each one (rate plans times nights times taxes);. rank them;. all within ~300 to 500 ms.

111
00:06:31.664 --> 00:06:38.661
At ~1,400 searches per second, that is ~1 to 5M property evaluations per second.

112
00:06:38.941 --> 00:06:44.893
You need precomputed availability and price summaries in memory, not database queries per property.

113
00:06:45.173 --> 00:06:46.645
ARI write volume.

114
00:06:46.925 --> 00:07:00.259
Each channel manager update (set rate for 2027 to 01 to 10 to 2027 to 01 to 20 to 6,500 rupees) can touch hundreds of cells.

115
00:07:00.539 --> 00:07:04.677
Millions of updates an hour means hundreds of millions of cell changes.

116
00:07:06.027 --> 00:07:10.742
The write path must be efficient: compact representations and range updates.

117
00:07:11.022 --> 00:07:12.259
Look-to-book ratio.

118
00:07:12.539 --> 00:07:15.398
Most traffic is search, so the read path dominates.

119
00:07:15.678 --> 00:07:22.121
You also need cheap, approximate answers for search, with exact checks at booking (§5.4).

