651
00:43:01.166 --> 00:43:02.510
Interview Q and A.

652
00:43:03.760 --> 00:43:04.592
Beginner.

653
00:43:04.872 --> 00:43:05.896
Question 1.

654
00:43:06.176 --> 00:43:09.056
How would you represent room availability for a hotel?

655
00:43:09.336 --> 00:43:10.701
Thirty-second answer:

656
00:43:10.981 --> 00:43:27.920
Per property, room type and date store inventory and sold counts, and per rate plan and date store prices by occupancy and restrictions (min stay, closed to arrival or departure); keep them as compact date-indexed arrays so a stay check is a min over the nights.

657
00:43:29.270 --> 00:43:30.273
Question 2.

658
00:43:30.553 --> 00:43:34.329
How do you prevent two guests booking the same homestay for the same dates?

659
00:43:34.609 --> 00:43:35.974
Thirty-second answer:

660
00:43:36.254 --> 00:43:51.635
The platform calendar is the source of truth; booking does a conditional write that blocks all dates only if all are free (optimistic concurrency or a per-listing lock); holds expire if payment fails; iCal and API sync to other sites.

661
00:43:52.985 --> 00:43:54.031
Question 3.

662
00:43:54.311 --> 00:43:57.660
Why does a search price sometimes differ from the booking price?

663
00:43:57.940 --> 00:43:59.305
Thirty-second answer:

664
00:43:59.585 --> 00:44:14.284
Search uses cached availability and price summaries for speed; between search and booking the hotel may change rates or sell rooms, so booking re-prices exactly and asks the user to accept changes, platforms track the mismatch rate.

665
00:44:15.634 --> 00:44:16.701
Question 4.

666
00:44:16.981 --> 00:44:19.669
How would you show a property's average rating fairly?

667
00:44:19.949 --> 00:44:21.314
Thirty-second answer:

668
00:44:21.594 --> 00:44:32.943
Use a Bayesian average that blends the property's ratings with a prior so properties with few reviews aren't over- or under-ranked; show sub-ratings and only verified-stay reviews.

669
00:44:34.293 --> 00:44:35.296
Intermediate.

670
00:44:35.576 --> 00:44:36.685
Question 5.

671
00:44:36.965 --> 00:44:40.656
Design hotel search that answers in under 500 ms.

672
00:44:40.936 --> 00:44:42.301
Thirty-second answer:

673
00:44:42.581 --> 00:45:01.824
Stage 1: geo and attribute index retrieves candidates; stage 2: scatter-gather to an in-memory availability or price cache sharded by property for stay checks and lead prices; stage 3: pricing (taxes, discounts) and ML ranking; cache popular queries briefly; log impressions.

674
00:45:03.174 --> 00:45:04.283
Question 6.

675
00:45:04.563 --> 00:45:08.531
How do ARI updates from channel managers flow through the system?

676
00:45:08.811 --> 00:45:10.177
Thirty-second answer:

677
00:45:10.457 --> 00:45:27.139
Partners push standard ARI messages to a connectivity gateway, which validates, maps IDs and writes to Kafka partitioned by property; the ARI service applies range updates with ordering checks; change events refresh the search cache within seconds.

678
00:45:28.489 --> 00:45:29.577
Question 7.

679
00:45:29.857 --> 00:45:32.908
What happens when a booking can't be delivered to the hotel?

680
00:45:33.188 --> 00:45:34.553
Thirty-second answer:

681
00:45:34.833 --> 00:45:50.535
Retry with backoff via the connectivity gateway, query status before re-sending to avoid duplicates, alert operations when undelivered past a threshold, and fall back to phone or email confirmation, an undelivered booking is a guest with no room.

682
00:45:51.885 --> 00:45:52.909
Question 8.

683
00:45:53.189 --> 00:45:56.389
How do cancellation policies affect the booking system?

684
00:45:56.669 --> 00:45:58.034
Thirty-second answer:

685
00:45:58.314 --> 00:46:15.679
Each rate plan or listing carries a policy (free until X, partial, non-refundable); the booking stores a snapshot of the policy at booking time; cancellation computes refunds from it, notifies the supplier, releases inventory and triggers refunds idempotently.

686
00:46:17.029 --> 00:46:18.096
Question 9.

687
00:46:18.376 --> 00:46:21.917
How do you detect duplicate properties from multiple suppliers?

688
00:46:22.197 --> 00:46:23.563
Thirty-second answer:

689
00:46:23.843 --> 00:46:37.965
Normalise names and addresses, compare geo distance, phone and website, use fuzzy name similarity and photo perceptual hashes, and merge high-confidence matches into a canonical property with multiple supply sources.

690
00:46:39.315 --> 00:46:40.083
Senior.

691
00:46:40.363 --> 00:46:41.409
Question 10.

692
00:46:41.689 --> 00:46:44.206
How would you build ranking for a stays marketplace?

693
00:46:44.486 --> 00:46:45.851
Thirty-second answer:

694
00:46:46.131 --> 00:46:55.838
Learning-to-rank on impression logs with bookings as positives and position-bias correction; features from listing quality, price competitiveness, reviews,

695
00:46:56.118 --> 00:47:10.134
query and user context; listing embeddings from click sessions for personalisation; online A or B tests on conversion with guardrails; business rules for sponsored labels, diversity and new-listing exploration.

696
00:47:11.484 --> 00:47:12.615
Question 11.

697
00:47:12.895 --> 00:47:15.433
How would you suggest prices to homestay hosts?

698
00:47:15.713 --> 00:47:17.079
Thirty-second answer:

699
00:47:17.359 --> 00:47:35.983
Model booking probability per night as a function of price using listing features, seasonality, lead time, local search demand and competitor prices; estimate elasticity from data and experiments; choose the price maximising expected revenue within host bounds; explain suggestions.

700
00:47:37.333 --> 00:47:38.421
Question 12.

701
00:47:38.701 --> 00:47:42.221
Why do hotels overbook, and how should an OTA handle it?

702
00:47:42.501 --> 00:47:43.866
Thirty-second answer:

703
00:47:44.146 --> 00:48:01.853
Hotels overbook to offset no-shows and cancellations, choosing a level where expected extra revenue exceeds expected walk cost; OTAs respect hotel settings, buffer scarce inventory, do live checks for last rooms, and have relocation workflows and partner scorecards.

704
00:48:03.203 --> 00:48:04.483
Question 13.

705
00:48:04.763 --> 00:48:08.325
How do you keep calendars in sync across multiple listing sites?

706
00:48:08.605 --> 00:48:09.971
Thirty-second answer:

707
00:48:10.251 --> 00:48:24.544
Prefer real-time API connections; otherwise iCal import and export with frequent polling for active listings, a short buffer for near-term dates, and conflict detection that alerts hosts and triggers rebooking workflows.

708
00:48:25.894 --> 00:48:27.089
Question 14.

709
00:48:27.369 --> 00:48:29.715
How would you detect fraud in stays bookings?

710
00:48:29.995 --> 00:48:31.361
Thirty-second answer:

711
00:48:31.641 --> 00:48:49.454
Payment risk scoring with device, IP, velocity, card-country mismatch, lead time and value; account takeover detection; message classifiers for off-platform payment requests; listing scam detection with photo hashes; step-up verification, holds and takedowns.

712
00:48:50.804 --> 00:48:51.700
Architect.

713
00:48:51.980 --> 00:48:53.175
Question 15.

714
00:48:53.455 --> 00:48:56.932
Design a hotel and homestay booking platform end to end.

715
00:48:57.212 --> 00:48:58.577
Thirty-second answer:

716
00:48:58.857 --> 00:49:15.540
Supply onboarding and content; ARI store with range updates fed by channel managers, extranet and hosts; two-stage search with geo index and in-memory availability or price cache; ML ranking and pricing; booking service with atomic holds,

717
00:49:15.820 --> 00:49:26.124
live checks where needed and idempotent payments; supplier delivery; payouts via ledger; reviews and trust; Kafka to a lakehouse for analytics and experiments.

718
00:49:27.474 --> 00:49:28.669
Question 16.

719
00:49:28.949 --> 00:49:33.130
How do you handle a 1000:1 look-to-book ratio economically?

720
00:49:33.410 --> 00:49:34.775
Thirty-second answer:

721
00:49:35.055 --> 00:49:50.309
Make the read path cheap: precomputed availability and price summaries in memory, brief query caching, rate-limited metasearch traffic, live supplier checks only at the property page or booking, and cost-aware caching for supplier APIs.

722
00:49:51.659 --> 00:49:53.003
Question 17.

723
00:49:53.283 --> 00:49:56.653
What data platform would you build for a travel marketplace?

724
00:49:56.933 --> 00:49:58.299
Thirty-second answer:

725
00:49:58.579 --> 00:50:17.139
Search impressions, clicks, bookings, ARI snapshots and prices into Kafka and a lakehouse; marts for funnels, supply health, price competitiveness and partner performance; ML for ranking, pricing, fraud and reviews; a large-scale experimentation platform with consistent assignment.

726
00:50:18.489 --> 00:50:19.662
Question 18.

727
00:50:19.942 --> 00:50:23.142
What compliance issues matter for an Indian stays platform?

728
00:50:23.422 --> 00:50:24.787
Thirty-second answer:

729
00:50:25.067 --> 00:50:48.171
GST on room tariffs and platform fees, TCS or TDS for e-commerce operators, guest registration (Form C for foreigners), card tokenisation and refund timelines, consumer protection e-commerce rules with grievance officers and transparent pricing, dark pattern guidelines and DPDP data protection, all to VERIFY against current rules.

730
00:50:49.521 --> 00:50:50.759
Question 19.

731
00:50:51.039 --> 00:50:56.287
How would you migrate from cached-only availability to live supplier checks for some partners?

732
00:50:56.567 --> 00:50:57.932
Thirty-second answer:

733
00:50:58.212 --> 00:51:15.897
Classify partners by price mismatch and sold-out rates, enable live checks at the property page and booking for the worst ones behind feature flags, add supplier caches and circuit breakers, measure conversion and latency in A or B tests, and push partners to faster ARI push.

734
00:51:17.247 --> 00:51:18.357
Question 20.

735
00:51:18.637 --> 00:51:21.069
Compare hotel booking with railway ticketing.

736
00:51:21.349 --> 00:51:22.714
Thirty-second answer:

737
00:51:22.994 --> 00:51:41.895
Both sell date-bound perishable inventory with holds and payments; railways have a single owner and extreme flash crowds (Tatkal) with quotas and waitlists, while hotels have distributed truth across channel managers, rich rate plans, ranking and pricing competition, and overbooking practices.

738
00:51:43.245 --> 00:51:44.291
Part 13.

739
00:51:44.571 --> 00:51:45.339
Summary.

740
00:51:46.589 --> 00:51:54.909
ARI (availability, rates and inventory per property times room type times rate plan times date) is the heart of the system.

741
00:51:55.189 --> 00:51:58.389
Store it compactly, with range updates and restrictions.

742
00:51:58.669 --> 00:52:06.327
Search is two-stage: geo and attribute retrieval, then in-memory availability and price summaries, then ML ranking.

743
00:52:06.607 --> 00:52:10.149
This makes a 1000:1 look-to-book ratio affordable.

744
00:52:10.429 --> 00:52:19.858
Booking is exact: re-price, place atomic holds or live supplier reservations, take idempotent payments, and deliver reliably to the hotel.

745
00:52:21.208 --> 00:52:23.021
Truth differs by supply type.

746
00:52:23.301 --> 00:52:29.040
The channel manager is the truth for hotels, which leads to sync lag and overbooking handling.

747
00:52:29.320 --> 00:52:34.227
The platform calendar is the truth for homestays, with iCal and API sync.

748
00:52:34.507 --> 00:52:48.800
Algorithms: date-range checks with arrays, bitmaps and prefix sums; learning-to-rank with embeddings; price suggestions from elasticity; overbooking control; Bayesian review averages; fraud and duplicate detection.

